CLAIM: step4 E1 pre-registration kept (JS)
  cite corpus/knowledge/mutation-case-kimi-census.md:183-191
     183: ## 5. Calibration and errata (append-only)
     184: 
     185: E1 — the author's pre-run expectation held for 9/10 defects; it was **wrong on D7**. The
     186: expectation filed D7 as a survivor (reasoning: "the loose fixtures lack the floor bits
     187: entirely, so both predicates fire"). Measured: both loose fixtures go CLEAN under D7 and
     188: the mutant dies on the two mode cases. The error was in the *reasoning about the fixture
     189: modes*, not the edit: credentials 0o500 vs floor 0o700 and server.token 0o400 vs floor
     190: 0o600 share bits with their floors, so `(mode & floor) === 0` admits them while
     191: `!== floor` rejects them (§3, D7). Recorded as a calibration miss.
    needle 'E1' -> present
    needle 'wrong on D7' -> present
    needle 'calibration miss' -> present

CLAIM: step4 E1 miscalled counts kept (bash)
  cite corpus/knowledge/mutation-case-kagi-census.md:184-191
     184: ## 5. Calibration and errata (append-only)
     185: 
     186: E1 — the pre-run expectation predicted the killed/survived verdict on **10/10** mutants but the
     187: failing-case *counts* on only 6/10: B2 (predicted 1, measured 2), B3 (predicted 2, measured 3),
     188: B6 (predicted 1, measured 2) all gained case 04 `runtime-absent`. Cause: I read case 04 as
     189: special-purpose, but it is a second exit-0 green fixture — the full clean tree minus one runtime
     190: row (`:770`) — so every defect that fires on green data kills it too. The miss was in reasoning
     191: about the fixtures, not the edits (same shape as round-2 E1).
    needle 'E1' -> present
    needle 'failing-case *counts* on only 6/10' -> present

